// TabPlayer's recorded drum kit: the Virtuosity Drums recordings, one key per kit sound, close and dry. The kick comes from the kick microphone (with the vintage-sounding
// microphone's recording of the same hit mixed in, see tp_pair), the snare, its cross stick and rim shot and the toms from the snare microphone, the hi-hats and cymbals from the
// overhead microphones; the percussion keeps the mid-distance microphone. Keys follow General MIDI, except 1 (snare rim shot) and 2 (half-open hi-hat), which General MIDI lacks.
// tabplayer-kit.processing.json beside this map names, per key, how the loader shapes each recording once when it loads it (companion mix, equaliser, compressor, tail envelope).
// tp_pair=<recording>: the second microphone's recording of the same hit. The loader aligns it in time and polarity with the main one and mixes it in; the prepare script pins it.
// Where the recordings sound their layers' dynamics themselves, amp_velcurve_1=1 keeps every layer at full level.
// volume is set after the processing, from the processed recordings: each sound is levelled to the General MIDI kit's loudness (K-weighted, first 300 ms, channel volume 119) at
// velocity 95, the Guitar Pro default dynamic, then offset (kick +1 dB, snare, cross stick and rim shot +2, hi-hats and cymbals -2, toms 0) and trimmed by 0.5 dB, which
// puts the metal pattern's whole-clip level on General MIDI's (docs/validation/2026-11-16-recorded-kit-balance.md). The kick, closed and open hi-hat, crash and ride list their
// regions here with a level for each recorded layer, because their layers are 6 to 15 dB apart, more than the velocity gain rises across one layer (4 to 7 dB); each layer is
// trimmed so the sound follows one smooth curve, 24 dB per decade of velocity.
// group/off_by: a voice of group G stops the voices whose off_by is G on the same track.

